00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.900
uh today we're going to talk about vector databases and why they matter

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:17.400
a lot of people um are confused about what they actually are

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.200
so let's start from the beginning and build up to production systems

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:24.700
first, what is an embedding? an embedding is a list of numbers

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:28.300
that represents the meaning of some content, like text or an image

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:30.400
a list of numbers that represents

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.800
the meaning of some content like text or an image

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:37.500
when you run text through a machine learning model, you get a vector

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:41.200
usually a few hundred to a few thousand dimensions long

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.600
the key property is that similar meaning ends up close in that space

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:49.400
so the word cat and the word kitten are near each other

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:53.100
while cat and carburetor are far apart

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:57.800
a vector database stores those vectors and answers one question fast

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:01.500
which vectors are closest to this query vector

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.200
that operation is called nearest neighbor search

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:08.900
and doing it exactly over millions of vectors is too slow

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:12.600
so real systems use approximate nearest neighbor, or ANN search

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.300
you trade a tiny bit of accuracy for a massive speedup

