00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:42.300
let's talk about indexing, because this is where the real engineering is

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:46.000
the most common index today is HNSW, hierarchical navigable small world

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:49.700
it builds a multi layer graph where each layer is a zoom level

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:53.400
search starts at the top with a few far apart nodes

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:57.100
and greedily walks down to find the approximate nearest neighbors

00:01:57.100 --> 00:02:00.800
graph based indexes give great recall but they use a lot of memory

00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:04.500
the other big family is inverted file, or IVF

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:08.200
IVF clusters the vectors with k means and only searches a few clusters

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:11.900
it's smaller and faster to build, but recall can be lower

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:15.600
you can also combine them: IVF first to pick clusters, then HNSW inside

00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:19.300
quantization is the third trick, shrinking each vector to fewer bits

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:23.000
product quantization can cut memory by eight times or more

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.700
with a small hit to accuracy that you can often tune back

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:30.400
so the index choice is really a trade between memory, speed and recall

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:34.100
and you should measure all three on your own data before shipping

00:02:34.100 --> 00:02:37.800
thanks for watching so far, if you're finding this useful

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:41.500
now let's move to production concerns, starting with latency budgets

00:02:41.500 --> 00:02:45.200
users expect answers in tens of milliseconds, not seconds

00:02:45.200 --> 00:02:48.900
so you need to profile every stage: encoding, search and reranking

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:52.600
the query encoder is often the hidden bottleneck

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:56.300
many teams cache popular query embeddings to cut that cost

00:02:56.300 --> 00:03:00.000
next, recall is a product decision, not just a metric

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.700
if recall drops, your retrieval augmented generation answers get worse

00:03:03.700 --> 00:03:07.400
so set a recall target, like ninety five percent at k equals ten

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:11.100
and alert when a deploy pushes it below that line

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:14.800
sharding matters once you pass a few million vectors

00:03:14.800 --> 00:03:18.500
you split the index across machines and fan out the query

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:22.200
then merge results by distance, which adds a tail latency risk

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:25.900
replication protects you from a single node failure

00:03:25.900 --> 00:03:29.600
keep at least one replica per shard and test failover regularly

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:33.300
finally, think about reindexing, because models get upgraded

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:37.000
a new embedding model means every vector must be recomputed

00:03:37.000 --> 00:03:40.700
plan for a blue green swap so users never see the migration

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:44.400
to summarize the whole video in one sentence

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:48.100
vector databases make meaning searchable by turning content into vectors

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:51.800
and answering nearest neighbor queries approximately but quickly

00:03:51.800 --> 00:03:55.500
pick your index by measuring memory, speed and recall on your data

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:59.200
and treat production as a discipline: budgets, targets and rehearsals

